  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T05:55:18.816Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nThe net taxable estate is computed by deducting allowable deductions from the gross estate, which includes all properties owned by the decedent at the time of death. Under the Tax Reform for Acceleration and Inclusion (TRAIN) Law, the estate tax rate is a flat 6% of the net taxable estate.\n\n### Interpretation\nThe gross estate includes all properties, real or personal, tangible or intangible, that the decedent owned at the time of death. Allowable deductions include funeral expenses, debts, and other expenses related to the estate. The net taxable estate is then subjected to a flat estate tax rate of 6%, simplifying the tax calculation process compared to previous progressive rates.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis means that for estate planning purposes, individuals should be aware that the total value of their estate will be taxed at a uniform rate of 6% after deductions. This flat rate can facilitate easier calculations and planning for heirs, as they can anticipate the tax liability more accurately without the complexities of a tiered tax structure.",
